  "docs/design/lease/lease.md": "# Lease Design\n\nModel validation contract: explicit-recording-v1\n\n## Introduction and Goals\n\nOwn the lease responsibility for the synthetic export customer project.\n\n## Context and Scope\n\nOwn the shared token contract; Worker is the consumer and System references this owner.\n\n## Architecture Constraints\n\nCustomer direction requires no stale worker commitment. The fixture assumes one database supporting atomic conditional updates; no deployed performance claim is made.\n\n## Requirements\n\n| ID | Required behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| LEASE-SR-01 | Renewal MUST preserve job and owner and increase generation; validation MUST reject stale or substituted lease identity before commitment. |\n\n## Solution Strategy\n\nAn atomic conditional transaction reads current job/owner/generation and validates the submitted token.\n\n## Building Block View\n\nOne local owner exposes its contract; shared validation refers to the Lease owner rather than copying its requirements.\n\n## Runtime View\n\nA request identifies its job and lease. A stale or unavailable authority stops commitment; the caller can retry after reacquiring a current lease.\n\n## Deployment View\n\nOne transactional store is a fixture assumption. Distribution and production installation are outside this synthetic project.\n\n## Crosscutting Concepts\n\nThe system model explains composition without overriding component rules.\n\n### Boundary scan and acceptance scenarios\n\n| Dimension | Requirement basis | Distinct outcome to demonstrate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Input domain | LEASE-SR-01 | Malformed token fails before commit. |\n| State/lifecycle | LEASE-SR-01 | Renewal preserves job/owner and increases generation. |\n| Identity/authority | LEASE-SR-01 | A substituted owner cannot authorize commitment. |\n| Composition/path | LEASE-SR-01 | Old worker plus renewed lease cannot jointly commit stale work. |\n| Temporal/retry | LEASE-SR-01 | Duplicate retry must revalidate current authority. |\n| Failure/recovery | LEASE-SR-01 | Unavailable authority yields no commit and a retryable result. |\n| Compatibility/migration | LEASE-SR-01 | Old token format is rejected under the declared fixture schema. |\n| External/environment | LEASE-SR-01 | Disconnected authority cannot establish current validity. |\n\nThe combined hazard is an individually valid worker retaining an obsolete lease. Integrated observation spans lease validation and the commit boundary, not merely the shape of a token.\n\n### Supporting examples\n\nexamples/before.json and examples/after.json are complete synthetic lease records governed by LEASE-SR-01 and schemas/lease-example.schema.json. They illustrate renewal for job-17/worker-a with initial generation1. examples/invalid-pair-after.json is a deliberate counterexample to unchanged job identity, not a permitted transition; both records individually meet the same schema.\n\n## Architecture Decisions\n\n| ID | Context and decision | Alternatives and consequences |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| LEASE-DEC-01 | Use the transactional authority at the commitment boundary to satisfy the no-stale-commit direction. | Cache-only authority is rejected because it cannot detect renewal. Network outage sacrifices availability and requires retry. |\n\n## Quality Requirements\n\nWhen authority is unavailable, commitment is absent and the client receives a retryable result. A walkthrough assesses the contract; Delivery allocates database and public command checks.\n\n## Risks and Technical Debt\n\nThe transaction capability is a declared assumption. The project owner must reconsider direction if it is unavailable.\n\n## Glossary\n\nLease: job/owner/generation tuple assessed against the current authority.\n\n## Next artifacts\n\nPlanning history: independent Design Review of exact affected owners and examples, followed by Delivery allocation under project authority. No review approval or execution status is asserted here.\n",
  "docs/design/worker/worker.md": "# Worker Design\n\nModel validation contract: explicit-recording-v1\n\n## Introduction and Goals\n\nOwn the worker responsibility for the synthetic export customer project.\n\n## Context and Scope\n\nConsume ../lease/lease.md LEASE-SR-01. Do not restate Lease token rules.\n\n## Architecture Constraints\n\nCustomer direction requires no stale worker commitment. The fixture assumes one database supporting atomic conditional updates; no deployed performance claim is made.\n\n## Requirements\n\n| ID | Required behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| WORKER-SR-01 | A worker MUST invoke Lease validation in the same transaction as job commitment and MUST make no commit when validation fails. |\n\n## Solution Strategy\n\nPass the token to its named Lease owner and commit only in the same successful transaction.\n\n## Building Block View\n\nOne local owner exposes its contract; shared validation refers to the Lease owner rather than copying its requirements.\n\n## Runtime View\n\nA request identifies its job and lease. A stale or unavailable authority stops commitment; the caller can retry after reacquiring a current lease.\n\n## Deployment View\n\nOne transactional store is a fixture assumption. Distribution and production installation are outside this synthetic project.\n\n## Crosscutting Concepts\n\nThe system model explains composition without overriding component rules.\n\n### Boundary scan and acceptance scenarios\n\n| Dimension | Requirement basis | Distinct outcome to demonstrate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Input domain | WORKER-SR-01 | Malformed token fails before commit. |\n| State/lifecycle | WORKER-SR-01 | Renewal preserves job/owner and increases generation. |\n| Identity/authority | WORKER-SR-01 | A substituted owner cannot authorize commitment. |\n| Composition/path | WORKER-SR-01 | Old worker plus renewed lease cannot jointly commit stale work. |\n| Temporal/retry | WORKER-SR-01 | Duplicate retry must revalidate current authority. |\n| F