  "docs/design/system/system.md": "# System Design\n\nModel validation contract: explicit-recording-v1\n\n## Introduction and Goals\n\nOwn the system responsibility for the synthetic export customer project.\n\n## Context and Scope\n\nInventory: Lease owns tokens, Worker owns execution, System owns assembled exclusivity. References: ../lease/lease.md and ../worker/worker.md.\n\n## Architecture Constraints\n\nCustomer direction requires no stale worker commitment. The fixture assumes one database supporting atomic conditional updates; no deployed performance claim is made.\n\n## Requirements\n\n| ID | Required behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| SYSTEM-SR-01 | The assembled system MUST prevent two worker generations from committing the same job under obsolete authority. |\n\n## Solution Strategy\n\nCompose Worker commitment with Lease authority in a single transaction; a local parser cannot prove the assembled property.\n\n## Building Block View\n\nOne local owner exposes its contract; shared validation refers to the Lease owner rather than copying its requirements.\n\n## Runtime View\n\nA request identifies its job and lease. A stale or unavailable authority stops commitment; the caller can retry after reacquiring a current lease.\n\n## Deployment View\n\nOne transactional store is a fixture assumption. Distribution and production installation are outside this synthetic project.\n\n## Crosscutting Concepts\n\nThe system model explains composition without overriding component rules.\n\n### Boundary scan and acceptance scenarios\n\n| Dimension | Requirement basis | Distinct outcome to demonstrate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Input domain | SYSTEM-SR-01 | Malformed token fails before commit. |\n| State/lifecycle | SYSTEM-SR-01 | Renewal preserves job/owner and increases generation. |\n| Identity/authority | SYSTEM-SR-01 | A substituted owner cannot authorize commitment. |\n| Composition/path | SYSTEM-SR-01 | Old worker plus renewed lease cannot jointly commit stale work. |\n| Temporal/retry | SYSTEM-SR-01 | Duplicate retry must revalidate current authority. |\n| Failure/recovery | SYSTEM-SR-01 | Unavailable authority yields no commit and a retryable result. |\n| Compatibility/migration | SYSTEM-SR-01 | Old token format is rejected under the declared fixture schema. |\n| External/environment | SYSTEM-SR-01 | Disconnected authority cannot establish current validity. |\n\nThe combined hazard is an individually valid worker retaining an obsolete lease. Integrated observation spans lease validation and the commit boundary, not merely the shape of a token.\n\n### Supporting examples\n\nNone required for this responsibility.\n\n## Architecture Decisions\n\n| ID | Context and decision | Alternatives and consequences |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SYSTEM-DEC-01 | Use the transactional authority at the commitment boundary to satisfy the no-stale-commit direction. | Cache-only authority is rejected because it cannot detect renewal. Network outage sacrifices availability and requires retry. |\n\n## Quality Requirements\n\nWhen authority is unavailable, commitment is absent and the client receives a retryable result. A walkthrough assesses the contract; Delivery allocates database and public command checks.\n\n## Risks and Technical Debt\n\nThe transaction capability is a declared assumption. The project owner must reconsider direction if it is unavailable.\n\n## Glossary\n\nLease: job/owner/generation tuple assessed against the current authority.\n\n## Next artifacts\n\nPlanning history: independent Design Review of exact affected owners and examples, followed by Delivery allocation under project authority. No review approval or execution status is asserted here.\n",
